<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Nri</h1><p class="st-meta">948 CE – 1911 CE (approx.) · capital Nri</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Igbo sacred kingdom whose priest-king, the Eze Nri, held ritual rather than military authority over a wide area of south-eastern Nigeria for nearly a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Nri traditions trace the kingdom to Eri, a culture hero said to have come down from the sky near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anambra-river">Anambra</a> river, and to his descendant Ifikuanim, remembered as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a>. The traditional founding date of 948 rests on genealogical reckoning. The famous 9th and 10th century bronzes from nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-ukwu">Igbo-Ukwu</a> are often linked to Nri ritual culture.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a> was a ritual ruler chosen after an interregnum and secluded in his palace. Nri did not rule by force: its titled priests, marked with ichi facial scars, travelled across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a> to cleanse abominations, crown local title-holders, settle disputes and proclaim peace. The Ozo title system and a network of yam and agricultural rites spread from Nri.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Nri's influence waned from the 18th century as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aro-confederacy">Aro Confederacy</a> and slave trading reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igboland">Igboland</a>. In 1911 British colonial officials forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eze-nri">Eze Nri</a> to renounce his ritual powers. The title survives as a traditional office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anambra-state">Anambra State</a>, and Nri is remembered as a cradle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/igbo-people">Igbo</a> culture.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="theocracy, 948 CE – 1911 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M30 82 V46 Q50 12 70 46 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 30